WEBVTT

NOTE Generated with faster-whisper base.en; source=videos/Core/VisualAcuity/VA_PEC1_220p.mp4; sourceLang=en; task=transcribe

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.178
This video demonstrates how to perform

2
00:00:04.178 --> 00:00:08.356
a distance and near visual acuity

3
00:00:08.356 --> 00:00:12.535
test.

4
00:00:12.535 --> 00:00:18.320
Wash your hands and don't PPE.

5
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.653
You will need the arclight charts and a safety pin to make pinholes.

6
00:00:22.653 --> 00:00:26.320
The room must be quiet and at least three metres wide.

7
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.320
It should be well lit ideally with natural light.

8
00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:34.243
If you're outside, make sure the sun is on the patient's back shining onto the chart.

9
00:00:34.243 --> 00:00:37.320
The patient should be seated comfortably although standing is okay.

10
00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.458
Show the patient the chart at arm's length, and ask them to point in

11
00:00:41.458 --> 00:00:42.640
the direction at the

12
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:43.451
ease point.

13
00:00:43.451 --> 00:00:47.504
Check the patient understands and you're both happy to start.

14
00:00:47.504 --> 00:00:48.720
If the patient

15
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.870
normally wears glasses for distance, use them for the test.

16
00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:54.945
If not, then test unaided.

17
00:00:54.945 --> 00:00:55.360
They

18
00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.245
should cover one eye with a pan with their hand, not their fingers.

19
00:00:59.245 --> 00:01:00.440
Place the chart at

20
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:04.792
the same level as the patient's eyes at a distance of 3 metres.

21
00:01:04.792 --> 00:01:06.800
Start with the largest letters at

22
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.040
the top of the chart working downwards.

23
00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.240
Stop the test when the patient can no longer correctly

24
00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.190
indicate the direction of the ease or they complete the test.

25
00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.440
Now record the acuity achieved.

26
00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.049
The left side of the chart is labelled in Snellin notation with log mar on the right.

27
00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:24.320
Snellin

28
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.707
is recorded as a fraction with a test distance on top and the last

29
00:01:28.707 --> 00:01:29.960
full line seen correctly

30
00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:30.929
on the bottom.

31
00:01:30.929 --> 00:01:34.160
If some of the next line was also seen then

32
00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.782
right plus one, or plus two, for extra letters seen.

33
00:01:37.782 --> 00:01:40.680
For a log mar again, record the last

34
00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:48.720
full line read correctly, but this time subtract 0.07 if they managed one extra letter correctly,

35
00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:51.036
or 0.14 if they saw two.

36
00:01:51.036 --> 00:01:56.440
If the patient cannot see even the biggest E at 3 metres, then half

37
00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:58.062
the distance to 1.5 metres.

38
00:01:58.062 --> 00:02:02.280
If they still cannot see at this distance, then test if they can

39
00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:10.054
count your fingers, follow your hand movements or if they can perceive light.

40
00:02:10.054 --> 00:02:11.250
Record what

41
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.437
they could see and the distance.

42
00:02:13.437 --> 00:02:17.810
If the patient's vision is reduced, less than 3 over 3 or

43
00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:20.456
log mar 0, then also test with a pinhole.

44
00:02:20.456 --> 00:02:23.690
This can show if the vision is reduced due to lack

45
00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:24.223
of glasses.

46
00:02:24.223 --> 00:02:28.490
You can make a set of pinholes by pushing a pin through the near card where

47
00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:29.654
the black dots are.

48
00:02:29.654 --> 00:02:33.730
As the patient to hold the card over the eye you are testing while

49
00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:35.597
covering their other eye with their palm.

50
00:02:35.597 --> 00:02:38.530
They will need to move it up or down a little

51
00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:41.162
so they can see through at least one hole.

52
00:02:41.162 --> 00:02:43.210
Repeat distance testing with the pinhole and

53
00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:48.730
record the results, making it clear that this test was with a pinhole.

54
00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:50.363
Now test near visual acuity.

55
00:02:50.363 --> 00:02:54.610
Ask the patient to hold the chart 40cm away at a typical reading

56
00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:55.003
angle.

57
00:02:55.003 --> 00:02:58.539
If they own reading glasses test with them on.

58
00:02:58.539 --> 00:03:00.504
If not, then test unaided.

59
00:03:00.504 --> 00:03:01.290
Ask them

60
00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.312
to keep both eyes open this time.

61
00:03:03.312 --> 00:03:06.490
Explain that you want them to read out loud, starting with

62
00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:08.930
the biggest letters at the top of the chart.

63
00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:11.370
If the patient cannot read well, then ask them

64
00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:15.883
to point to small words or certain letters such as O, name letters in

65
00:03:15.883 --> 00:03:16.850
the words, match

66
00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:22.479
letters to a matching card or copy the shapes of letters in the ear with their finger.

67
00:03:22.479 --> 00:03:22.810
Stop

68
00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:27.034
the test when the patient can no longer read accurately and comfortably.

69
00:03:27.034 --> 00:03:28.090
Record the number

70
00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:30.454
of the last line they saw clearly.

71
00:03:30.454 --> 00:03:34.170
If their near vision is worse than N8, then repeat with

72
00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:36.250
reading glasses of different powers.
